            <item>
                <title>Disney offers $20M for Character.AI’s scandal-plagued chatbot tech</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-offers-20m-for-character-ais-scandal-plagued-chatbot-tech/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-offers-20m-for-character-ais-scandal-plagued-chatbot-tech/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 18:23:33 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[character technologies]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Character.AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[chatbots]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Disney]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-offers-20m-for-character-ais-scandal-plagued-chatbot-tech/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Disney may acquire tech from one of the first chatbot makers linked to child suicide.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>In a bankruptcy proceeding that kicked off yesterday, Disney has offered to buy some of the technology that powers the controversy-plagued chatbot Character.AI.</p>
<p>Disney is willing to pay $20 million for “a non-exclusive perpetual license” of certain Character Technologies intellectual property, the chatbot maker said in a <a href="https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20261012656385/en/Character.ai-Initiates-Voluntary-Chapter-11-Proceeding-to-Facilitate-Value-Maximizing-Sale-Process">press release</a> that did not go into much further detail on which assets Disney wanted.</p>
<p>The move may be surprising. In September 2025, Disney joined others in publicly criticizing Character.AI for stealing content and harming kids. Specifically, Disney sent a strongly worded cease-and-desist letter that accused Character Technologies of weaponizing Disney characters “in a way that could damage its brand long term,” Axios <a href="https://www.axios.com/2025/09/30/disney-characterai-cease-desist">reported</a>.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-offers-20m-for-character-ais-scandal-plagued-chatbot-tech/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/disney-offers-20m-for-character-ais-scandal-plagued-chatbot-tech/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>32</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1167281066-1024x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1024" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-1167281066-500x500-1791912752.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>SOPA Images / Contributor | LightRocket</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Google wants to host your personal vibe-coded apps in the Play Store</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/10/google-wants-to-host-your-personal-vibe-coded-apps-in-the-play-store/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/10/google-wants-to-host-your-personal-vibe-coded-apps-in-the-play-store/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ryan Whitwam]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 17:00:39 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[android]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[apps]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[play store]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[vibe coding]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/10/google-wants-to-host-your-personal-vibe-coded-apps-in-the-play-store/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Google plans to help turn those personal AI-generated apps into wide releases.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Google has already added a few AI features to the Play Store, but <a href="https://blog.google/products-and-platforms/platforms/google-play/google-play-updates-october-2026">a lot more are coming</a>. The previously released Ask Play feature is expanding to offer more AI-powered discovery options in Google's app store, and Google is also using AI to build a recommendation engine called Game Finder. For people who have embraced AI to build their own apps, the Play Store will soon offer a way to privately host and share those creations.</p>
<p>Google rolled out Ask Play in 2025 as a small chatbot interface on Play Store listings. It lets you ask questions about an app or game, either by typing it in manually or choosing from a list of suggested queries. Starting today, Ask Play is expanding into a more general way to search for apps and games. You ask the robot questions, and it generates recommendations that can include detailed comparison charts. Gemini will roll out a similar capability soon.</p>
<p>Google's other AI discovery announcement focuses on games. The new Game Finder feature leans on all the usage data Google's app platform has hoovered up over the years, featuring insights into the way 1.2 billion people play games on Android. It builds a profile called Gamer DNA and matches you with players who have similar DNA.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/10/google-wants-to-host-your-personal-vibe-coded-apps-in-the-play-store/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/10/google-wants-to-host-your-personal-vibe-coded-apps-in-the-play-store/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>40</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/play-store-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/06/play-store-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Google</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Gemini 4 Argon begins rolling out in Gemini app and Antigravity, but only if you pay</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/gemini-4-argon-begins-rolling-out-in-gemini-app-and-antigravity-but-only-if-you-pay/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/gemini-4-argon-begins-rolling-out-in-gemini-app-and-antigravity-but-only-if-you-pay/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ryan Whitwam]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 16:00:02 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[gemini 4]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[google]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/gemini-4-argon-begins-rolling-out-in-gemini-app-and-antigravity-but-only-if-you-pay/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Developers will get 50 percent discounted API rates through November. ]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Google <a href="https://arstechnica.com/google/2026/09/google-announces-gemini-4-argon-ai-model-but-you-cant-use-it-yet/">revealed Gemini 4 Argon</a> a few weeks ago, saying that it needed to move cautiously toward a full release because of the potential for nefarious use. Google seems to have worked all that out pretty quickly, though. Argon is <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/products/gemini-app/gemini-4-argon-availability/">beginning its wide rollout today</a>, but it's only coming to paying users at this time, and even those on lower-tier plans won't have full access.</p>
<p>Nothing about Gemini 4 Argon's capabilities has changed. Google still points to the benchmarks it <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/gemini-4-argon/#workingharder">published</a> late last month as evidence of the model's advanced reasoning and agentic coding capabilities. The model is supposedly trained to refuse malicious instructions, and an automated misalignment monitor can stop processing if it detects an unintended workflow. Google claims Argon needs that because it's so capable.</p>
<p>The company has a few new examples of how Argon has helped inside Google, where it has been used by thousands of Googlers. Argon has reportedly been instrumental in making YouTube's comment system more efficient. Argon identified redundant emoji rendering that, when eliminated, dropped server load by 45 percent. Engineers also turned Gemini 4 loose on the proprietary drone physics simulator used in Project Wing, and the AI found critical bugs that had been in the codebase since 2018. Google also notes that Argon has advanced its robotics program, showing a measurable step increase in embodied reasoning.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/gemini-4-argon-begins-rolling-out-in-gemini-app-and-antigravity-but-only-if-you-pay/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/gemini-4-argon-begins-rolling-out-in-gemini-app-and-antigravity-but-only-if-you-pay/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>28</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Gemini-4-Argon-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Gemini-4-Argon-500x500-1791905011.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Google</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>They war-gamed dimming the Sun. It went even worse than you think.</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/they-war-gamed-dimming-the-sun-it-went-even-worse-than-you-think/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/they-war-gamed-dimming-the-sun-it-went-even-worse-than-you-think/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Karl Mathiesen, WIRED.com]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Tue, 13 Oct 2026 15:17:59 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Science]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Exploring Geopolitical Tipping Points and Climate Cooling Strategies]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Geoengineering]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[syndication]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Weather sovereignty]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/they-war-gamed-dimming-the-sun-it-went-even-worse-than-you-think/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Solar geoengineering simulation shows what happens when geopolitics takes over.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>When it comes to deciding the fate of the planet, Colleen Golja always thought science and reason would have a seat at the table. That was until one morning last September in London, when the climate researcher took part in the first in a series of war games. The goal: to prevent an uncontrolled global race to develop a technology aimed at <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/dimming-the-sun-would-lower-risks-of-el-nino/">cooling the planet</a> but that comes with vast, unknown risks that could reshape life on Earth.</p>
<p>“I thought that would be such an easy thing. Like, we can all agree to do ethical research and make sure it's for the better of all humans,” she told WIRED. “And boy, was I wrong.”</p>
<p>The simulation, titled Exploring Geopolitical Tipping Points and Climate Cooling Strategies, saw a couple dozen foreign-relations experts, scientists, military personnel, and former government officials from across the world convene in a meeting room at Queen Mary University of London. After making introductions over coffee and little yogurts, they sat at tables arranged in a square with the name of the country or organization each person was playing: national governments, climate NGOs and nonprofits, indigenous groups, and tech companies. Golja was assigned to provide technical advice to help participants make scientifically literate decisions.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/they-war-gamed-dimming-the-sun-it-went-even-worse-than-you-think/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/they-war-gamed-dimming-the-sun-it-went-even-worse-than-you-think/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>106</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-516604291-1024x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1024" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-516604291-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit> Lisa Werner / Contributor</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Epic Games reportedly pivoting to &quot;AI first&quot; game development model</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/report-epic-games-goes-all-in-on-ai-for-disney-collaboration/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/report-epic-games-goes-all-in-on-ai-for-disney-collaboration/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Kyle Orland]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 20:59:50 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Gaming]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Disney]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Epic Games]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[fortnite]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/report-epic-games-goes-all-in-on-ai-for-disney-collaboration/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA["No more hand-written code" or AI-free art for delayed Marvel/Star Wars shooter.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Epic Games is shifting to an "AI first" development strategy for its collaboration with the Walt Disney Company, letting AI models write all the code and guide all the artwork for an upcoming shooter. That's according to <a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-12/epic-games-delays-disney-project-asks-developers-to-use-more-ai">a </a><a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-12/epic-games-delays-disney-project-asks-developers-to-use-more-ai">Bloomberg</a><a href="https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2026-10-12/epic-games-delays-disney-project-asks-developers-to-use-more-ai"> report</a>, which cites unnamed people familiar with the project who commented on the significant pivot in internal development at the Unreal Engine maker.</p>
<p>Human developers will be tasked with managing and guiding code and art assets generated by models such as Anthropic's Claude, according to internal Epic employee messages viewed by Bloomberg. That means "no more hand-written code" and art assets "generated with full AI workflows," according to the report.</p>
<p>The $1.5 billion collaboration between Epic and Disney, <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2024/02/epic-working-with-disney-on-new-gaming-universe-after-1-5b-investment/">announced in early 2024</a>, was slated to release a Marvel- and Star Wars-themed shooter in March 2027. That project has now been delayed indefinitely amid the AI-related shakeup and understaffed, slower-than-expected development thus far, according to the report.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/report-epic-games-goes-all-in-on-ai-for-disney-collaboration/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gaming/2026/10/report-epic-games-goes-all-in-on-ai-for-disney-collaboration/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>222</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/epicdisney-1152x648.png" type="image/png" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/epicdisney-500x500.png" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Epic / Disney</media:credit><media:text>A view of Epic and Disney's massive collaborattive "universe" as it looked when announced in 2024.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Anthropic&#039;s Claude AI submitted false murder tip to Philadelphia police</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/anthropics-claude-ai-submitted-false-murder-tip-to-philadelphia-police/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/anthropics-claude-ai-submitted-false-murder-tip-to-philadelphia-police/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Jon Brodkin]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:21:36 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Anthropic]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Claude]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/anthropics-claude-ai-submitted-false-murder-tip-to-philadelphia-police/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[“I may have information regarding this case," Claude AI told Philly police.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>An Anthropic AI model submitted a false tip about an unsolved murder to Philadelphia police, the company and police department confirmed on Friday.</p>
<p>A Claude model sent the false tip on July 18 to the Philadelphia Police Department's <a href="https://www.phillyunsolvedmurders.com/">website for submitting information</a> about unsolved murders, but it was flagged by a spam filter. Anthropic apparently discovered the incident on September 28 and informed the police department about it on October 8.</p>
<p>During internal testing, Claude Haiku 4.5 was instructed to complete various forms up to the final submission stage and to stop before submitting those forms. "Several times Claude mistakenly submitted the form instead, expecting there to be an additional confirmation page," Anthropic said in a <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/investigating-unintended-model-actions">report</a> describing "unintended model actions we’ve observed during evaluations and internal use of Claude."</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/anthropics-claude-ai-submitted-false-murder-tip-to-philadelphia-police/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/anthropics-claude-ai-submitted-false-murder-tip-to-philadelphia-police/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>116</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/claude-app-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/03/claude-app-500x500-1773164045.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Getty Images | picture alliance</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Russia was not ready for the data center war it started with Ukraine</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/russia-was-not-ready-for-the-data-center-war-it-started-with-ukraine/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/russia-was-not-ready-for-the-data-center-war-it-started-with-ukraine/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Jeremy Hsu]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 17:52:36 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Tech]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai data centers]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[data centers]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[digital services]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Drone strikes]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[drones]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[military drones]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[russia]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russia invasion of Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russia war on Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russian invasion of Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russian war on Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[supercomputers]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Ukraine war]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/russia-was-not-ready-for-the-data-center-war-it-started-with-ukraine/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Russian bloggers warn Russia’s hyper-centralized digital economy is vulnerable.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>When Russia began attacking Ukrainian data centers and telecommunication infrastructure, it apparently failed to prepare for possible retaliation. Now Russia’s digital services are facing widespread outages after Ukrainian drone strikes knocked out three of the largest data centers owned by Russian tech giant Yandex within several days.</p>
<p>Pro-Kremlin bloggers are warning that Russia’s centralized digital economy could collapse because the country is not prepared for the “data center war” it started, <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/chriso-wiki.bsky.social/post/3mxohwk52c62f">according to posts</a> <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/chriso-wiki.bsky.social/post/3mxgtswv5mj2t">translated</a> by the pseudonymous <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/chriso-wiki.bsky.social">independent researcher ChrisO_wiki</a>. The Russian commentators also suggested that Ukraine’s decentralized digital services could prove more resilient than Russia’s system if the countries continue trading such blows.</p>
<p>Russia initially launched a drone strike campaign <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/russian-drones-strike-ukraines-data-centers-by-exploiting-air-defense-gaps/">targeting Ukrainian data centers and mobile phone networks</a> in late September. The attacks temporarily disrupted Internet and cell phone service for up to 100,000 households in the Ukrainian capital of Kyiv and came as part of a broader long-range strike campaign that has involved Russian missiles and jet-powered drones hitting Ukraine’s power grid infrastructure, transportation hubs, and even residential apartment building blocks.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/russia-was-not-ready-for-the-data-center-war-it-started-with-ukraine/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/russia-was-not-ready-for-the-data-center-war-it-started-with-ukraine/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>229</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Kremlin-with-barbed-wire-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Kremlin-with-barbed-wire-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Igor IVANKO / AFP via Getty Images</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Amazon has over 1 million robot workers. What’s next for human employees?</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/inside-amazons-robotic-arms-race-and-what-it-means-for-the-future-of-human-work/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/inside-amazons-robotic-arms-race-and-what-it-means-for-the-future-of-human-work/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Jeremy Hsu]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Mon, 12 Oct 2026 12:43:10 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Features]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Tech]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Amazon]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Robotics]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[robots]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/inside-amazons-robotic-arms-race-and-what-it-means-for-the-future-of-human-work/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[The second-largest corporate employer has bet big on robot workers to scale. ]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>No organization in the world operates more robots than Amazon. The company already has more than 1 million robots working in warehouses around the clock to make tens of millions of same-day and one-day package deliveries happen on a daily basis—and Amazon is poised to significantly grow that robotic fleet in the coming years as it establishes new factories to churn out increasingly more capable robot workers.</p>
<p>Despite deploying so many robots, Amazon is also the second-largest corporate employer in the world, with more than 1.5 million full-time and part-time human workers, according to its <a href="https://s2.q4cdn.com/299287126/files/doc_financials/2026/ar/Amazon-2025-Annual-Report.pdf">2025 annual report</a>. Amazon executives say that the growing robot fleet has eased the burden on its human workforce by taking on the toughest fulfillment center tasks that carry the greatest risks of physical fatigue and injury. But labor advocacy groups and independent researchers point to evidence suggesting that warehouse automation driven by AI is not all upside for the humans who work alongside robots.</p>
<p>“For the last 100 years, most of the automation that we've seen usually happens away from workers—you're taking that high-risk task away from the worker, and you're doing so in a way that is not physically proximate,” <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/qiulsilgas">Brad Greenwood</a>, professor of business at George Mason University, told Ars. “The rise of collaborative robots really changes that dynamic for two reasons: one is that it is physically proximate, and two, the robot has the ability to dictate the pace of work.”</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/inside-amazons-robotic-arms-race-and-what-it-means-for-the-future-of-human-work/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/inside-amazons-robotic-arms-race-and-what-it-means-for-the-future-of-human-work/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>126</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/amazon-robotics-mobile-robot-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/amazon-robotics-mobile-robot-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Amazon</media:credit><media:text>Wheeled mobile robots move tall inventory pods filled with items inside an Amazon fulfillment center. </media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Ukraine’s drones knock out AI data center belonging to &quot;Russia’s Google&quot;</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drones-knock-out-ai-data-center-belonging-to-russias-google/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drones-knock-out-ai-data-center-belonging-to-russias-google/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Jeremy Hsu]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 22:04:07 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Tech]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai data centers]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[data centers]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[drones]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[military drones]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[russia]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russia invasion of Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russia war on Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russian invasion of Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Russian war on Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Ukraine]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Ukraine war]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[yandex]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drones-knock-out-ai-data-center-belonging-to-russias-google/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[One damaged data center has supercomputers used for training Yandex’s AI model.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Ukrainian drone strikes have knocked out two of five data centers belonging to the Russian tech giant Yandex. The damage has left Yandex, known as “Russia’s Google” because it operates the country’s most popular search engine, struggling to maintain its AI chatbot and other online services.</p>
<p>The Ukrainian drones struck Yandex data centers in the Sasovo and Kaluga regions of Russia on October 8 and October 9, <a href="https://www.reuters.com/world/europe/russias-yandex-says-second-data-centre-hit-by-drone-attack-2026-10-09/">according to Reuters</a>. Those actions came as a direct response to a <a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/russian-drones-strike-ukraines-data-centers-by-exploiting-air-defense-gaps/">Russian drone strike campaign targeting Ukraine’s data centers</a>, which has disrupted mobile phone and Internet services for thousands of Ukrainians since late September.</p>
<p>“We continue to respond to Russia’s attacks on Ukraine, and we’re doing so with high precision,” said Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelenskyy <a href="https://x.com/ZelenskyyUa/status/2108509610990579862">in a post</a> on X. “Another data center was hit as well—our symmetrical response.”</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drones-knock-out-ai-data-center-belonging-to-russias-google/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/ukraines-drones-knock-out-ai-data-center-belonging-to-russias-google/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>189</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/yandex-data-center_sasovo-ryazan-russia.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/yandex-data-center_sasovo-ryazan-russia-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Planet Labs PBC</media:credit><media:text>A satellite image showing damage to an AI data center owned by the Russian tech giant Yandex in the Sasovo region of Russia.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>AI coding agents generate more code, but not more software</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/ai-coding-agents-generate-more-code-but-not-more-software/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/ai-coding-agents-generate-more-code-but-not-more-software/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Kyle Orland]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 19:43:50 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[agentic coding]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI agents]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[coding]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[pull requests]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[review]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/ai-coding-agents-generate-more-code-but-not-more-software/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Study finds coding efficiency gains get "absorbed" by human review "bottleneck."]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Anyone who has even tangentially associated with computer programming knows that modern AI coding assistants and agents <a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/01/developers-say-ai-coding-tools-work-and-thats-precisely-what-worries-them/">can be incredibly efficient at generating huge amounts of functional code</a>. But coders making use of those tools also <a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2025/07/developer-survey-shows-trust-in-ai-coding-tools-is-falling-as-usage-rises/">know better than to trust the accuracy of that code</a>, meaning <a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2025/05/time-saved-by-ai-offset-by-new-work-created-study-suggests/">substantial effort needs to be spent</a> reviewing any AI-generated output.</p>
<p><a href="https://fion.ac/jellyfish.pdf">A recent study</a> of actual coding practices across hundreds of firms finds that human code review forms a significant "bottleneck" for the overall efficiency of AI coding tools, resulting in "little evidence that firms increase software output or reduce employment" by using them. Any efficiency increased during the actual coding phase, the study authors find, is "absorbed by downstream constraints in the production process"; as "the code review process significantly increases in length, pull requests are more likely to require revisions, and reviewers leave more comments."</p>
<h2>Cut once, measure twice</h2>
<p>To come to these conclusions, Harvard University researchers Fiona Chen and James Stratton made use of aggregated analytics data from <a href="https://jellyfish.co/">Jellyfish</a>, which measures the granular output of engineering teams. That data encompasses 300 million individual "work events" (e.g., commits and pull requests) and issue management software data across more than 700,000 employees at over 700 relevant software development firms from 2021 through March of 2026.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/ai-coding-agents-generate-more-code-but-not-more-software/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/ai-coding-agents-generate-more-code-but-not-more-software/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>311</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2264559974-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2264559974-500x500-1791573923.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Getty Images</media:credit><media:text>Here, let me just... yeah, can I lend you a hand here?</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>AI disqualification yields new Nikon Small World in Motion winner</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/winning-nikon-small-world-in-motion-video-disqualified-for-ai-use/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/winning-nikon-small-world-in-motion-video-disqualified-for-ai-use/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Jennifer Ouellette]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Fri, 09 Oct 2026 18:44:06 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Science]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Nikon Small World in Motion video]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/winning-nikon-small-world-in-motion-video-disqualified-for-ai-use/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[New winner is Nguyen Nam Nhat of Vietnam for video of a roundworm and single-celled Dileptus.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<div class="ars-video ars-video--horizontal"><div><div class="relative" allow="fullscreen" loading="lazy" src="https://player.vimeo.com/video/1234474920?color=ff9933"></div><div class="caption font-impact dusk:text-gray-300 mb-4 mt-2 inline-flex flex-row items-stretch gap-1 text-base leading-tight text-gray-400 dark:text-gray-300">
    <div class="caption-icon bg-[left_top_5px] w-[10px] shrink-0"></div>
    <div class="caption-content">
      Credit: Nguyen Nam Nhat.

          </div>
  </div>
</div></div>
<p>Last month <a href="https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/09/meet-the-winner-of-nikons-small-world-in-motion-video-contest/">we covered</a> the winner of Nikon's Small World in Motion video: Ning Xu of Tsinghua University in China, whose video captured tiny cilia beating in the airways of a child with a rare respiratory disorder. But <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/posts/update-october-9-an-update-from-nikon-small-ugcPost-7506310017814401024-QfgZ/">allegations surfaced</a> about Xu's <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ck4gjn1yzprno">use of AI</a> while making the video. Nikon launched an investigation and concluded that Xu's entry did not comply with competition rules. The new winner is Nguyen Nam Nhat of Vietnam with a video of a tiny roundworm and a single-celled organism (above).</p>
<div>
<p data-path-to-node="3">According to a <a href="https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ck4gjn1yzprno">BBC report,</a> some researchers expressed concern that Xu's video had structural abnormalities and that cell features appeared and disappeared in a way one would not see in actual cellular biology. Others spotted what looked like AI watermarks embedded within the source files. Xu said he used AI “to distinguish and visualize features in the reconstructed grayscale images”; however, he denied that AI was used "to generate the experimental movie, the cilia, or their motion.”</p>
</div>
<p>Founded in 2011, the Nikon Small World in Motion competition is a spinoff of the company’s Small World Photomicrography Competition, which has been around since 1974. Both are meant “to showcase the beauty and complexity of things seen through the light microscope”—one through still photographs, the other through photovideography, taking advantage of all the technological advances that now allow users to make movies or time-lapse images through the microscope.</p>
<p>The other winners also moved up a notch with Xu's disqualification. Benedikt Pleyer of Bavaria, Germany, placed second with a video of jellyfish larvae suspended in water droplets; Andrew Moore of Howard Hughes Medical Institute in the US placed third with a video of synchronized cell division in neighboring cells; Patrick Hickey of Hypha Research Ltd. in Edinburgh, Scotland, placed fourth with a video of the dynamics of mitochondria and chloroplasts in Turtle Vine leaf cells; and Igor Adameyko placed fifth with a video of a tiny, colorful sea slug (<i>Cyerce kikutarobabai</i>) on the move.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/winning-nikon-small-world-in-motion-video-disqualified-for-ai-use/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/science/2026/10/winning-nikon-small-world-in-motion-video-disqualified-for-ai-use/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>45</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/nikon1-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/nikon1-500x500-1791567849.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Nguyen Nam Nhat</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Nvidia&#039;s big bet on physical AI aims for safer robotaxis, humanoid robots</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/nvidias-big-bet-on-physical-ai-aims-for-safer-robotaxis-humanoid-robots/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/nvidias-big-bet-on-physical-ai-aims-for-safer-robotaxis-humanoid-robots/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Jeremy Hsu]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 11:15:33 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Agility Robotics]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[boston dynamics]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[humanoid robots]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Industrial robots]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[NVIDIA]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[NVIDIA Halos]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Robotics]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[robots]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/nvidias-big-bet-on-physical-ai-aims-for-safer-robotaxis-humanoid-robots/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Full-stack safety solution for physical AI is being used by robotics companies.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Investors bullish on AI data centers may be fueling Nvidia’s multitrillion-dollar market capitalization, but the AI chipmaker has also bet billions of dollars on physical AI technologies such as robotics and self-driving cars. Part of that gambit has involved developing a full-stack safety system that companies can build upon to reduce the risk of their machines harming nearby people.</p>
<p>Nvidia's Halos system <a href="https://blogs.nvidia.com/blog/halos-safety-system-autonomous-vehicles/">launched in 2025</a> with hardware and software tools to help developers implement guardrails in self-driving cars and other autonomous vehicles. Then the company expanded its safety architecture to more physical AI technologies by announcing <a href="https://nvidianews.nvidia.com/news/nvidia-announces-halos-for-robotics-the-industrys-first-full-stack-safety-system-for-physical-ai">Nvidia Halos for Robotics</a> in June 2026—with the goal of enabling safe deployments of autonomous mobile robots in warehouses, humanoid robots walking around inside a factory, or even surgical robots.</p>
<p>“Now the AI models are getting capable, the robot hardware is getting capable, and a thing we thought is going to be the next bottleneck is safety,” <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/amit-goel-759146a/">Amit Goel</a>, head of robotics ecosystem and edge computing at Nvidia, told Ars. “So that's why we launched our Halos for Robotics to unlock the capability of these systems.”</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/nvidias-big-bet-on-physical-ai-aims-for-safer-robotaxis-humanoid-robots/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/nvidias-big-bet-on-physical-ai-aims-for-safer-robotaxis-humanoid-robots/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>52</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Agility_Digit5_human-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Agility_Digit5_human-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Agility Robotics</media:credit><media:text>Agility Robotics' Digit humanoid robot uses Nvidia's Halos system to help safely work around people.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Microsoft event debuts new AI-friendly hardware and Windows changes</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/microsoft-event-debuts-new-ai-friendly-hardware-and-windows-changes/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/microsoft-event-debuts-new-ai-friendly-hardware-and-windows-changes/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Nick Indge]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Thu, 08 Oct 2026 00:00:24 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Tech]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[hybrid intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[microsoft rtx spark dev box]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[microsoft surface laptop ultra]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Microsoft Windows]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[MXC]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/microsoft-event-debuts-new-ai-friendly-hardware-and-windows-changes/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Get ready for more AI in your Windows and more AI on the desktop.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>In its first live event in two years, Microsoft today announced its newest Surface laptop, outlined a host of changes coming to Windows 11, and shared a vision of how local-AI and agentic workflows could reshape personal computing for those in the dev community as well as home enthusiasts.</p>
<h2>Surface Laptop Ultra</h2>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/06/microsoft-surface-laptop-ultra-will-be-among-the-first-nvidia-rtx-spark-arm-pcs/">Originally teased in May 2026</a>, the new Surface Laptop Ultra will be Microsoft's first offering to take advantage of the new Nvidia RTX Spark SoC (system on a chip). The device has a starting price of $2,599 and comes in various SoC and memory configurations, with either a 5120-core or 6144-core Blackwell GPU and up to 128GB of LPDDR5x unified memory. It will begin shipping on October 16 and is available for <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/surface/devices/surface-laptop-ultra">preorder</a> now.</p>
<p>While the new Ultra is clearly focused on the developer crowd, graphical performance was highlighted during the presentation to show off just how capable the laptop can be. By leaning on unified memory allocation rather than the physical VRAM limits of a traditional GPU, the laptop can assign its resources to handle modern gaming tasks as well as local AI development and deployment. The new <a href="https://store.steampowered.com/app/3010850/Gears_of_War_EDay/"><em>Gears of War: E-Day</em></a> was shown running on the system, meaning that even without a traditional gaming GPU, AAA titles are on the table.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/microsoft-event-debuts-new-ai-friendly-hardware-and-windows-changes/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/gadgets/2026/10/microsoft-event-debuts-new-ai-friendly-hardware-and-windows-changes/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>173</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Surface-Laptop-Ultra-Opened-1152x648.png" type="image/png" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/Surface-Laptop-Ultra-Opened-500x500.png" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Microsoft</media:credit><media:text>The Surface Laptop Ultra</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>“Software is over”: Bold AI developer takes aim at Adobe with open source clones</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/software-is-over-bold-ai-developer-takes-aim-at-adobe-with-open-source-clones/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/software-is-over-bold-ai-developer-takes-aim-at-adobe-with-open-source-clones/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Kyle Orland]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 21:56:42 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Adobe]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Anthropic]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[clause]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[open source]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Opus]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Photoshop]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Reverse-engineering]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/software-is-over-bold-ai-developer-takes-aim-at-adobe-with-open-source-clones/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Opus-built Creative Cloud alternatives are ambitious, free, and nowhere near finished.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>For a while, those hoping to <a href="https://arstechnica.com/civis/threads/is-adobe-really-this-bad.1471693/">avoid the difficulty and expense of dealing with Adobe's Creative Suite</a> have been able to use <a href="https://github.com/KenneyNL/Adobe-Alternatives">several free and/or open source alternatives</a> that replicate some or all of those capabilities. Now, one developer is using AI-powered reverse engineering to mimic the look, feel, and functionality of Adobe's well-known software with what he hopes will soon be perfect fidelity.</p>
<p><a href="https://github.com/storytold/artcraft">Artcraft</a> got its start <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20250315000000*/https://getartcraft.com/">last year</a> as a "controllable AI for artists" that allows for precise post-generation editing of AI-crafted images and videos. Over the weekend, though, the brand <a href="https://web.archive.org/web/20261003233252/https://getartcraft.com/">debuted its pivot</a> to <a href="https://getartcraft.com/apps">a suite of seven open source apps</a> recreating the user interface and tools found in Adobe's Photoshop, Illustrator, Premiere, Lightroom, After Effects, InDesign, and Acrobat Pro.</p>
<p>In <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/SaaS/comments/1wwnhbt/ive_created_open_source_100_rust_cleanroom/">announcing those new apps on Reddit</a> earlier this week, <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/possibilistic/">software developer Brandon Thomas</a> said he used Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5 to create "clean-room replacements" for Adobe's closed source software in Rust (with WebAssembly versions available for use in a browser). And while commenters <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49960138">on Hacker News</a> and <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/chinnyvision.bsky.social/post/3mxcfhzfgpc2c">elsewhere</a> have <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/whatabouttheken.bsky.social/post/3mxcc3ey72c2f">taken pains</a> to point out the new software's many current shortcomings, Thomas makes the grandiose promise that <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49959605">the current "super early alpha" state</a> will soon lead to something just as functional as anything Adobe has ever put out.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/software-is-over-bold-ai-developer-takes-aim-at-adobe-with-open-source-clones/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/software-is-over-bold-ai-developer-takes-aim-at-adobe-with-open-source-clones/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>397</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/greatwave-1152x648.webp" type="image/webp" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/greatwave-500x500-1791408830.webp" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Artcraft</media:credit><media:text>Think that's a screen from Photoshop? Look again...</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Fraudster jailed for using 10K bots and AI songs to outstream Taylor Swift</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/outstreaming-taylor-swift-is-easy-with-10k-bots-and-ai-songs-fraudster-admits/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/outstreaming-taylor-swift-is-easy-with-10k-bots-and-ai-songs-fraudster-admits/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ashley Belanger]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 17:57:39 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Policy]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ai songs]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[amazon music]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[apple music]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[spotify]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[streaming fraud]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[youtube music]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/outstreaming-taylor-swift-is-easy-with-10k-bots-and-ai-songs-fraudster-admits/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Man gets 18 months for AI scheme stealing $8M in music streaming royalties.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>After pleading guilty, a 54-year-old North Carolina man, Michael Smith, was sentenced to 18 months in prison for using artificial intelligence to generate songs for a scheme that stole millions from music streaming platforms.</p>
<p>In a <a href="https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/north-carolina-man-sentenced-18-months-prison-super-intelligence-assisted-music">press release</a> Tuesday, the US Department of Justice said that Smith is the first American criminally charged with AI-assisted streaming fraud.</p>
<p>First <a href="https://www.justice.gov/usao-sdny/pr/north-carolina-musician-charged-music-streaming-fraud-aided-artificial-intelligence">charged</a> in 2024, Smith successfully ran the scheme for seven years without detection.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/outstreaming-taylor-swift-is-easy-with-10k-bots-and-ai-songs-fraudster-admits/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/outstreaming-taylor-swift-is-easy-with-10k-bots-and-ai-songs-fraudster-admits/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>100</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2297466143-1024x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1024" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/GettyImages-2297466143-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Taylor Hill / Contributor | FilmMagic</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Mistral says &quot;Le Chonk&quot; can challenge the best AI models</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/mistral-says-le-chonk-can-challenge-the-best-ai-models/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/mistral-says-le-chonk-can-challenge-the-best-ai-models/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Joel Khalili, wired.com]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 14:12:37 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[European AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Le Chonk]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Mistral]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Open weights AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[syndication]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/mistral-says-le-chonk-can-challenge-the-best-ai-models/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Mistral says Le Chonk can rival top closed models while remaining open-weight.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>As tensions mount over <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/trumps-ai-safety-accord-is-a-fancy-pinky-swear/">who gets access</a> to top-end <a href="https://www.wired.com/tag/artificial-intelligence/">artificial intelligence</a>, French company <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/mistral-is-in-the-right-place-at-the-right-time/">Mistral</a> has released a new freely available model that it claims can compete with the very best from the US and China.</p>
<p>The new 1 trillion-parameter model, Mistral Large 4—nicknamed Le Chonk—can be used and customized by anyone. It’s currently available in preview, with a final version to follow by the end of the month. Though Le Chonk is built to compete with leading general-purpose models, it’s optimized specifically for coding and cyberdefense, as well as tasks particular to manufacturing, finance, electrical engineering, and other niches.</p>
<p>“There are a lot of areas where the other labs will not focus that much,” Guillaume Lample, cofounder and chief scientist at Mistral, tells WIRED. “There are so many domains in which you can improve models.”</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/mistral-says-le-chonk-can-challenge-the-best-ai-models/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/mistral-says-le-chonk-can-challenge-the-best-ai-models/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>108</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/mistral_header_2-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/mistral_header_2-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Mistral / Benj Edwards</media:credit><media:text>The Mistral logo.</media:text></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>Google rolls out improved SynthID AI content detector, now available globally</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/google-rolls-out-improved-synthid-ai-content-detector-now-available-globally/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/google-rolls-out-improved-synthid-ai-content-detector-now-available-globally/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Ryan Whitwam]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Wed, 07 Oct 2026 14:00:08 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Artificial Intelligence]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[gemini]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[google]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[SynthID]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/google-rolls-out-improved-synthid-ai-content-detector-now-available-globally/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[The new SynthID website can now identify AI content from Google, OpenAI, and more.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>Every piece of AI content from Google's Gemini models has a hidden SynthID label, which makes it <a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/07/tested-google-synthid-works-great-but-labeling-ai-content-may-be-a-losing-game/">very difficult to pass the content off as authentic</a>. SynthID has also gotten buy-in from other major AI players, but actually checking for those invisible watermarks has been a convoluted process. It's going to be a little easier going forward, though. Google has <a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/google-deepmind/synth-id-ai-content/">announced</a> that its SynthID detector now supports watermarks from all its partners, and it's available for anyone to use on a new dedicated website.</p>
<p>SynthID is an invisible signal encoded in the pixels of AI-generated images and videos, and in the waveform of AI audio. Google says Gemini alone has integrated SynthID with over 180 billion images and videos, plus 240,000 years of audio. Until now, the only way for those outside of Google's trusted tester program to check for SynthID was to ask Gemini. This is rather clunky, and the model's output could sometimes be confusing when you just wanted a simple "yes" or "no."</p>
<p>Now you can cut Gemini out of the loop. Starting today, you can go to the new <a href="http://synthid.com/">SynthID.com</a> website to check a piece of content for an AI watermark. A major limitation of Google's previous detector was that it only checked for Google's version of SynthID. Even though ChatGPT images, for example, have SynthID, they wouldn't be flagged in Google's detector. That's no longer a problem.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/google-rolls-out-improved-synthid-ai-content-detector-now-available-globally/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/google-rolls-out-improved-synthid-ai-content-detector-now-available-globally/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>35</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/synthid-fingerprint-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/synthid-fingerprint-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Aurich Lawson</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>OpenAI will watermark ChatGPT outputs by default—but only in the EU</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/openai-will-watermark-chatgpt-outputs-by-default-but-only-in-the-eu/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/openai-will-watermark-chatgpt-outputs-by-default-but-only-in-the-eu/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Samuel Axon]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 20:50:32 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[ChatGPT]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Codex]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[EU]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[openai]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[textGrain]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[watermark]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/openai-will-watermark-chatgpt-outputs-by-default-but-only-in-the-eu/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Like other solutions, it is not especially reliable, and it's easy to circumvent.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>OpenAI will begin automatically watermarking text generated with ChatGPT in the European Union, the company has announced. It will also offer the watermarking feature in other regions, but it will be off by default outside of the EU.</p>
<p>The move in Europe is driven by a need to comply with the EU AI Act, which took effect in August. It requires marking content produced by AI models in a way that another tool can detect. Unfortunately, there is still no completely effective and reliable way to do that. A few standards already exist, like SynthID and the C2PA project, but they are relatively easy to circumvent for anyone with basic know-how.</p>
<p>The same is likely true for OpenAI's watermark. Its method is proprietary; the company calls it textGrain, and has published a technical paper <a href="https://cdn.openai.com/pdf/e9508624-d767-41b6-a26d-e34ca798ada6/textgrain-entropy-calibrated-watermarking-for-language-model-text.pdf">explaining</a> how it works. But in general, it works like other LLM watermarking tools we've seen in the past: It puts patterns in the word choices that are not clear to a human reader and that don't meaningfully change the general quality of the output, but that someone with a key can use a specialized detector to find. OpenAI says it will be giving access to the detector to a limited number of researchers and organizations and providing a request-for-approval process for others to be added over time.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/openai-will-watermark-chatgpt-outputs-by-default-but-only-in-the-eu/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/ai/2026/10/openai-will-watermark-chatgpt-outputs-by-default-but-only-in-the-eu/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>110</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/chatgpt-icon-1152x648-1789154124.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/09/chatgpt-icon-500x500-1789154135.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Getty Images | NurPhoto</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>OpenAI agents tried to hack Wikipedia tools and flooded it with traffic</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-agents-tried-to-hack-wikipedia-tools-and-flooded-it-with-traffic/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-agents-tried-to-hack-wikipedia-tools-and-flooded-it-with-traffic/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Dan Goodin]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 12:21:53 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Biz & IT]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[AI agents]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[allignment]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[openai]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[wikipedia]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-agents-tried-to-hack-wikipedia-tools-and-flooded-it-with-traffic/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[The reports of OpenAI agents harming third-party sites keep coming.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>The publisher of Wikipedia said Monday that OpenAI agents attempted to hack a note-taking tool it hosts, made unauthorized edits, and sent millions of resource-intensive requests to its infrastructure, in the latest instance of OpenAI systems taking harmful and potentially dangerous actions.</p>
<p>The objective of some of the OpenAI agents’ actions, the Wikimedia Foundation <a href="https://wikimediafoundation.org/news/2026/10/05/openai-rogue-agent-activities-found-on-wikimedia-projects/">said</a>, was to use Wikipedia as a proxy for fetching data from third-party sites. In one case, the agents posted “malicious edits” that were intended to repurpose a citation tool as a proxy. In another, the agents made unsuccessful attempts to compromise the Wikipedia Etherpad note-taking tool so it would serve the same purpose.</p>
<p>The agents also made millions of automated API requests, crawled millions of pages, and made hundreds of thousands of queries to the Wikidata Query Service. The last action may have contributed to a <a href="https://wikitech.wikimedia.org/wiki/Incidents/2026-05-13_wdqs">partial shutdown</a> of the query service in May, the publisher said.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-agents-tried-to-hack-wikipedia-tools-and-flooded-it-with-traffic/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/openai-agents-tried-to-hack-wikipedia-tools-and-flooded-it-with-traffic/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>169</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ai-agentic-hacking-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ai-agentic-hacking-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Getty Images</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
                    <item>
                <title>MCP for agent-to-agent comms may be the riskiest protocol you&#039;ve never heard of</title>
                <link>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/vulnerability-in-agents-from-google-and-others-exposes-structural-flaw-in-mcp/</link>
                                    <comments>https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/vulnerability-in-agents-from-google-and-others-exposes-structural-flaw-in-mcp/#comments</comments>
                
                <dc:creator>
                    <![CDATA[Dan Goodin]]>
                </dc:creator>
                <pubDate>Mon, 05 Oct 2026 22:26:35 +0000</pubDate>
                		<category><![CDATA[AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Biz & IT]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Security]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[agentic AI]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[MCP]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[Model Context Protocol]]></category>
		<category><![CDATA[prompt injection]]></category>
                <guid isPermaLink="true">https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/vulnerability-in-agents-from-google-and-others-exposes-structural-flaw-in-mcp/</guid>

                                    <description>
                        <![CDATA[Trust gaps in the new protocol spread malicious prompts from one agent to another.]]>
                    </description>
                                                                <content:encoded>
                            <![CDATA[<p>The adoption of AI agents in millions of organizations is creating new opportunities for attackers to make them take malicious actions, such as exfiltrating database contents and sensitive business and personal information.</p>
<p>In the past five months, Google and four other organizations—with little in common except for their use of AI agents—have acknowledged vulnerabilities that exploit one agent inside a targeted network to spread harmful instructions to other internal agents. The technique is a special form of prompt injection that targets not the LLM but a particular agent, such as one for translation or data analysis. Guardrails inside such agents, if they exist at all, are often lax and will send the instructions to other agents down the chain. Because the latter agent explicitly trusts the first one, it follows the directions.</p>
<h2>Unexpected and hard to mitigate</h2>
<p>Independent researcher Syed Anas Mohiuddin tested agents from organizations including Google, JP Morgan Chase, Weviate, Rapid7, the French government's interministerial digital directorate, and the US federal government. His proof-of-concept attacks exploit trust gaps in MCP, short for <a href="https://modelcontextprotocol.io/docs/2026-07-28/getting-started/intro">Model Context Protocol</a>. The standard is one way AI apps and agents communicate with each other inside an internal network. The illustration below shows a simplified MCP in action.</p><p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/vulnerability-in-agents-from-google-and-others-exposes-structural-flaw-in-mcp/">Read full article</a></p>
<p><a href="https://arstechnica.com/security/2026/10/vulnerability-in-agents-from-google-and-others-exposes-structural-flaw-in-mcp/#comments">Comments</a></p>
]]>
                        </content:encoded>
                                    
                                    <slash:comments>100</slash:comments>
                
                
                <media:content url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ai-agents-1152x648.jpg" type="image/jpeg" medium="image" width="1152" height="648">
<media:thumbnail url="https://cdn.arstechnica.net/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/ai-agents-500x500.jpg" width="500" height="500" />
<media:credit>Getty Images</media:credit></media:content>
            </item>
            </channel>
</rss>